# Post-Independence India: The Republic's Journey Since 1947

> Midnight of 15 August 1947 began the world's largest experiment:
> could a poor, diverse, colonised subcontinent run itself as one
> democratic republic? This lesson tracks the republic's milestones —
> integration, wars, green and white revolutions, the Emergency,
> liberalisation, the nuclear and digital ages.

## 1. Consolidating the Union (1947–56)

- **Partition** — division of Punjab and Bengal; ~10–15 million
  displaced; Gandhi's assassination (30 January 1948).
- **Integration of 562 princely states** — **Sardar Vallabhbhai
  Patel and V. P. Menon**'s masterwork: Instrument of Accession by
  15 August 1947 for almost all; Junagadh and Hyderabad by 1948
  (police action, Operation Polo); **Kashmir's accession** (October
  1947) followed by the first India–Pakistan war (1947–48) and the
  UN-brokered ceasefire line.
- **Constitution** (26 November 1949 adopted; **26 January 1950
  in force**) — drafted under **Dr B. R. Ambedkar**, the world's
  longest written constitution; universal adult franchise from day
  one.
- **Linguistic reorganisation** — Potti Sriramulu's fast led to
  Andhra State (1953); **States Reorganisation Act, 1956** redrew
  the map on linguistic lines (exceptions later: Bombay split 1960,
  Punjab 1966, the north-east series, and 2000's three new states:
  Jharkhand, Chhattisgarh, Uttarakhand; Telangana 2014).

## 2. The Nehru Era: Planning the New India (1947–64)

[definition]
**Nehruvian consensus:** parliamentary democracy, **secularism**,
**non-alignment** in foreign policy, and a **planned, public-sector-
led mixed economy** aimed at self-reliance — symbolised by the
**Five-Year Plans** modelled on Gosplan but democratic.
[/definition]

- **Planning:** Planning Commission (1950); First FYP (Harrod-Domar,
  agriculture) → Second (1956, **Mahalanobis model**: heavy industry,
  steel plants at Bhilai, Rourkela, Durgapur), dams as "temples of
  modern India" — Bhakra-Nangal, Hirakud, Damodar Valley Corporation;
  IITs (first Kharagpur, 1951), AIIMS (1956), UGC (1956).
- **Foreign policy**: Panchsheel (1954); **Bandung (1955)** and the
  founding of the **Non-Aligned Movement** (Belgrade 1961, with
  Nasser and Tito; Nehru, Nkrumah, Sukarno); **Hindi-Chini bhai
  bhai** collapsing into the **1962 China war** — a strategic shock.
- **Goa, Daman and Diu liberated (1961)** from Portugal.

## 3. Wars, Shastri, and Indira (1964–77)

| Year | Event | Outcome |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 1965 | Second war with Pakistan | Tashkent Agreement (Kosygin); Shastri dies Tashkent 1966 |
| 1966 | Indira Gandhi becomes PM; **green revolution begins** (Borlaug's HYV wheat, M.S. Swaminathan) | Food self-sufficiency by mid-1970s (Punjab, Haryana, west UP) |
| 1967 | **Panchayati Raj** era local polls; Naxalbari rising | — |
| 1969 | **Congress split; 14 banks nationalised** | Political economics tilts left |
| 1971 | **Bangladesh Liberation War**; Pakistan defeated, 93,000 POWs; Simla Agreement 1972 | India's decisive strategic win |
| 1974 | **Pokhran-I "Smiling Buddha"** — first nuclear test (Raja Ramanna, Homi Bhabha's programme) | The nuclear threshold |
| 1975–77 | **The Emergency** — Allahabad HC unseats Indira; internal Emergency declared 25 June 1975; press censorship, MISA detentions, forced sterilisations | Democratic darkest hour; repealed March 1977; **Janata Party wins 1977** — first non-Congress government |

## 4. The White Revolution and the Coalitions (1977–91)

- **Operation Flood (1970–96)** — **Verghese Kurien**, Anand
  (Gujarat), the **Amul** cooperative; India becomes the largest milk
  producer ("national dairy development board," village
  cooperatives).
- 1980: Indira Gandhi returns; 1984 — **Operation Blue Star**,
  Indira's assassination (31 October), **Rajiv Gandhi** landslides;
  telecom and computer seeds (C-DOT, Sam Pitroda), national
  education policy 1986, **Shah Bano** and **Bofors** controversies;
  1987 **Sri Lanka IPF deployment**; Assam Accord (1985), Punjab
  accord (Rajiv–Longowal).
- 1989–91: V. P. Singh's National Front — **Mandal Commission**
  (OBC reservations implemented 1990), Chandra Shekhar; Rajiv's
  assassination (1991, Tamil Tigers).

## 5. 1991: The Liberalisation Turning Point

[important]
The 1991 **balance-of-payments crisis** (reserves down to ~$1
billion — two weeks of imports; pawned gold) forced reform. PM
**P. V. Narasimha Rao** and Finance Minister **Dr Manmohan Singh**:
rupee devaluation, industrial delicensing, trade liberalisation,
disinvestment — dismantling the Licence Raj and launching two
decades of 6–8% growth.
[/important]

## 6. The Contemporary Republic (1991–present)

- **1998**: Pokhran-II nuclear tests (Vajpayee) — declared nuclear
  power; **Kargil War (1999)** victory (Operation Vijay); bus
  diplomacy (Lahore 1999), Agra 2001.
- **Economy**: IT boom (Bangalore, Hyderabad), services-led growth;
  Golden Quadrilateral highways; RTI (2005), **MGNREGA (2005)** —
  world's largest work programme; **Right to Education (2009)**;
  Aadhaar (2010, Nandan Nilekani), GST (2017), **Insolvency and
  Bankruptcy Code (2016)**, Swachh Bharat, Digital India, UPI's
  payment revolution.
- **Polity**: coalition era (United Front, NDA-I, UPA-I/II, NDA-II
  and the return of single-party majorities 2014 and 2019; 2024
  coalition NDA-III), first woman President (Pratibha Patil, 2007),
  **Article 370's special status ended (2019)** — J&K and Ladakh
  made union territories; women's reservation act (2023);
  Chandrayaan-3's lunar south-pole landing (2023) and the G20 Delhi
  summit.
- **Challenges**: population, inequality, federal frictions,
  climate commitments (net-zero 2070), water stress and the long
  unfinished agendas of health, education and jobs.

[summary]
Since 1947 India integrated 562 princely states (Patel), adopted
Ambedkar's constitution (1950), reorganised states linguistically
(1956), planned a mixed economy (Nehru, dams, IITs) while founding
non-alignment, then passed through the 1962 shock, green revolution,
bank nationalisation, the 1971 victory and Pokhran-I, the Emergency
and Janata interregnum, Operation Flood's white revolution, Mandal
politics, and the 1991 Rao–Singh liberalisation into today's
nuclear, digital, space-faring G20 economy — democracy's largest,
loudest, ongoing experiment.
[/summary]
